  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThe central idea is to use a multi-resolution learning approach combined with physics-inspired optimization. The optimization attempts to minimize the overlap integral C_5 by:\n   - Using a three-stage process: coarse global search, medium-term refinement, and final detailed tuning\n   - Incorporating information from multiple grid resolutions to escape local optima\n   - Merging benefit across resolutions using interpolation and upscaling\n\nBottlenecks addressed include:\n   - Local optimization traps in the parent's approach\n   - Inefficient handling of grid transitions\n   - Slow convergence for high resolution\n   - Late plateauing: improvement only slows down later\n\nDifferent from the parent's iterative binary optimization or continuous projection, this approach uses:\n   - Nested resolution optimization\n   - Rotation-based gradient computation for faster correlation calculation\n   - Skipping hopeless searches early\n   - Fine-grained performance accounting per resolution\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nRepresentation: \n   - h(x) defined for x in [0,2] as a step function\n   - Represented discretely with n_points samples: x_i = i * dx, dx = 2/(n_points)\n   - Binary values h[i] in {0,1} with sum(h) * dx = 1 exactly\n\nObjective:\n   C_5 = max_k integrate_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n   Computed discretely as:\n      (2/n_points) * max_k (sum_i h[i] * (1-h[i+k])) * dx (handled by finite differences)\n\nConstraints:\n   0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i\n   sum(h) * dx = 1\n\nCorrelation Calculation:\n   Use gradient-based automatic differentiation with a rotation convolution that better captures local behavior at boundaries.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\nPhase 0: Setup and initialization\n   - Validate n_points even\n   - Initialize parent if provided, else generate coarse initial candidate\n   - Set global objective to measure pm max correlation\n   - Cross-fertilize between resolutions using scale factors\n\nPhase 1: Coarse grid optimization\n   - Perform binary-hybrid search over grid with 3n_points resolution\n   - Use rotation-invariant correlation calculation\n   - Early convergence test: if max correlation stops improving, reduce budget\n   - Budget allocation: 40% maximum of available time\n\nPhase 2: Medium grid refinement\n   - Move to 2n_points resolution grid\n   - Run gradient-based continuousNesterov optimization\n   - Apply early Termination if fusion fails\n   - Budget allocation: 30% maximum available\n\nPhase 3: Final binary-polish\n   - Operate at original n_points resolution with local refinements\n   - Budget allocation: remaining time after phase 1+2 complete\n\nAcceptance Criteria:\n   - New candidate must show at least 1.0% improvement in C_5\n   - Gradient-based phases must meet statistical significance test\n\nResolving parent-child mapping:\n   - Use projection if resolution changes: \n        old -> new: linear interpolation scaling\n   - Preserve boundary conditions through reflection padding\n\n4. Search choices and variations\nDefault settings:\n   - Base resolution: 380 (from parent) \n   - Tested resolutions: 400, 500, 550\n   - Medium-scored resolution delta: 1.0 to 1.5%\n   - C_5 improvement threshold: 0.0001% absolute reduction\n\nVariations:\n   - Random restart: 10% of bases cases replenished randomly\n   - Temperature schedule: exponential drop every 200 iterations\n   - Twin approach: run two optimization lines in parallel at 0.6% performance cost\n   - Active schedule: adaptive based on improvement rate\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nEstimates:\n   - Resolution increase exponents: time \u2248 O(n_points^(1/4)) due to sparse gradient\n   - FFT operations still approximate to O(n_points log n_points)\n   - Benefit-find: early termination reduces worst-case to 40% of full time\n   - Parallel restart: requires negligible extra time vs improvement\n\nTermination:\n   - Check nested deadlines at each phase start/end\n   - Use comparative mean of correlation values\n   - Fallback: return parent-like solution if no improvement after 800s\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   - Validate binary values and sum\n   - Recompute C_5 to ensure no floating point issues\n   - Perform redundant checks for circular overlaps\n   - Include outlier detection for corrupted candidates\n\nEnforcement of constraints:\n   - Clip values per iteration\n   - Resample if cumulative error > 0.1%\n   - Use calibration procedure with multiple resampling bins\n\nFeasibility checks:\n   - sum(h) in [n_points/2 - 0.5, n_points/2 + 0.5]\n   - All values in [0,1]\n\nOutput format:\n   - Preserve original dimensions from parent\n   - Return with updated resolution if isolated improvement\n   - Avoid dimension mishaps by fallback projection",
